Description
Comprehensive documentation for the beast-credential-registry repository, detailing its mission to replace state-run licensing boards and bureaucratic gatekeeping with verifiable on-chain credentials, architecture details, and Google Cloud Run deployment instructions.

Core Code
Markdown
# BEAST Credential Registry (`beast-credential-registry`)

`beast-credential-registry` is a decentralized identity and professional licensing division under the New World Order Private Government framework. It provides a sovereign, tamper-proof alternative to state-run licensing boards, replacing institutional gatekeeping and arbitrary fees with verifiable on-chain credentials and peer-attested skill badges.

---

## 🏗 Repository Architecture

```text
beast-credential-registry/
├── contracts/               # Solidity smart contracts for credential issuance
│   └── CredentialRegistry.sol # On-chain identity, licensing, and revocation logic
├── backend/                 # Node.js Express API & PostgreSQL database integration
│   ├── src/                 # API routes, database connectors, and credential handlers
│   ├── db/                  # SQL schema definitions
│   └── Dockerfile           # Cloud Run container configuration
├── frontend/                # Next.js App Router portal for issuers and credential holders
├── .github/workflows/       # CI/CD deployment pipeline to Google Cloud Run
└── README.md                # System documentation
🚀 Getting Started
1. Prerequisites
Node.js (>= 20.0.0)

PostgreSQL / Google Cloud SQL instance

MetaMask or compatible Web3 wallet

2. Run Backend Service
Bash
cd backend
npm install
npm run dev
3. Run Frontend Portal
Bash
cd frontend
npm install
npm run dev
🪪 Core Principles
Sovereign Identity: Eliminates reliance on monopolistic state licensing boards and bureaucratic approval bottlenecks.

Verifiable Proofs: Cryptographic badges and credentials that can be instantly verified on-chain by any community member or peer group.

Decentralized Revocation: Transparent authority controls ensuring credentials remain accurate, immutable, and fully under issuer-holder consensus.
